¶
'''thick-lipped''' (thik′lipt), ''a.'' Having thick lips, as a negro; labroid, as a fish; thickened around the edges, as an ulcer.—'''Thick-lipped perch.''' See ''perch''<sup>1</sup>.

'''thicklips''' (thik′lips), ''n.'' A person having thick lips—a characteristic of the negro race: used opprobriously.

{{smaller block|What a full fortune does the ''thick-lips'' owe,<br>If he can carry 't thus!
{{right|''Shak.'', Othello, i. 1. 66.}}}}

'''thickly''' (thik′li), ''adv.'' In a thick manner, in any sense of the word ''thick;'' densely; closely; deeply; abundantly; frequently.

'''thickness''' (thik′nes), ''n.'' [< ME. ''thiknesse'', < AS. ''thicnes'', < ''thicce'', thick: see ''thick''.] 1. The state or property of being thick, in any sense; specifically, that dimension of a solid body which is at right angles both to its length and to its breadth; the third or least dimension of a solid.

{{smaller block|Sex fyngre thicke a floore thereof thou pave<br>With lyme and asshes mixt with cole and sande,<br>A flake above in ''thiknesse'' of thyne hande.
{{right|''Palladius'', Husbondrie (E. E. T. S.), p. 13.}}}}

{{smaller block|The height of one pillar was eighteen cubits; . . . and the ''thickness'' thereof was four fingers.
{{right|Jer. lii. 21.}}}}

2. That which is thick; the thick of anything; the dense, heavy, deep, or solid part.

{{smaller block|The chambers were in the ''thickness'' of the wall of the court toward the east.
{{right|Ezek. xlii. 10.}}}}

{{smaller block|This enormous ''thickness'' of nearly three miles of Old Red Sandstone.
{{right|''J. Croll'', Climate and Cosmology, p. 270.}}}}

readings.c1s2:
3. A fold, layer, or sheet, as of cloth or paper.—4. In ''founding'', the sand or loam placed temporarily in a mold while it is being prepared for casting. It is afterward removed, and its place is filled with the molten metal.

'''thickness''' (thik′nes), ''v. t.'' [< ''thickness'', ''n.''] To reduce to a uniform thickness before dressing to shape: said of boards and timber. [Trade use.]

'''thick-pleached''' (thik′plēcht), ''a.'' Thickly interwoven.

{{smaller block|The prince and Count Claudio, walking in a ''thick-pleached'' alley in my orchard, were thus much overheard by a man of mine.
{{right|''Shak.'', Much Ado, i. 2. 10.}}}}

'''thick-set''' (thik′set), ''a.'' and ''n.'' '''I.''' ''a.'' 1. Set, growing, or occurring closely together; dense; luxuriant.

{{smaller block|His eyeballs glare with fire, suffus'd with blood;
His neck shoots up a ''thick set'' thorny wood.
{{right|''Dryden'', tr. of Ovid's Metamorph., viii., Meleager and [Atalanta, l. 23.}}}}

{{smaller block|Live long, ere from thy topmost head
The ''thick-set'' hazel dies.
{{right|''Tennyson'', Will Waterproof.}}}}

2. Thickly studded; abounding; plentifully supplied.

{{smaller block|With windows of this kind the town of Curzola is ''thick-set'' in every quarter.
{{right|''E. A. Freeman'', Venice, p. 214.}}}}

3. Heavily or solidly built; stout; especially, short and stout.

{{smaller block|At Grantham, I believe, he sat up all night to avoid sleeping in the next room to a ''thick-set'' squinting fellow, in a black wig and a tarnished gold-laced waistcoat.
{{right|''Scott'', Rob Roy, iii.}}}}

readings.c1s3:
¶
{{smaller block|Laying a short, ''thickset'' finger upon my arm, he looked up in my face with an investigating air.
{{right|''Bulwer'', Pelham, xxxvi.}}}}

{{smaller block|'''Thick-set cord''', a kind of thick-set of which the surface is ribbed like that of corduroy.}}

'''II.''' ''n.'' '''1.''' A close or thick hedge.—'''2.''' Very thick or dense underwood; bush; scrub.—'''3.''' A kind of fustian having a nap like that of velveteen. It is used for clothes by persons engaged in manual work.

'''thick-sighted''' (thik′sī″ted), ''a.'' Dim of sight; weak-sighted.

{{smaller block|Whereas before she could see some furniture in her house, now she could perceive none; she was erst ''thick-sighted'', but now purblind. {{right|''Rev. T. Adams'', Works, I. 388.}}}}

'''thickskin''' (thik′skin), ''n.'' and ''a.'' '''I.''' ''n.'' One who has a thick skin—that is, one who is insensible to or not easily irritated by taunts, reproaches, ridicule, or the like; a rude, unimpressible person.

{{smaller block|The shallowest ''thick-skin'' of that barren sort.
{{right|''Shak.'', M. N. D., iii. 2. 13.}}}}

'''II.''' ''a.'' Same as ''thick-skinned.''

{{smaller block|Nor can I bide to pen some hungry scene<br>For ''thick-skin'' ears, and undiscerning eyne.
{{right|''Bp. Hall'', Satires, i. 8.}}}}

'''thick-skinned''' (thik′skind), ''a.'' '''1.''' Having a thick skin or rind: as, a ''thick-skinned'' animal; a ''thick-skinned'' orange.—'''2.''' Specifically, in ''zoöl.'', pachydermatous, as a rhinoceros; belonging to the ''Pachydermata.''—'''3.''' Insensible to reproach, ridicule, or insult; dull; stolid.

{{smaller block|He is too ''thick-skinned'' to mind eloquent and indignant criticism. {{right|''The American'', IX. 387.}}}}

