readings.c2s2:
E. also ''theef''; < ME. ''theef'', ''thef'' (pl. ''theeves'', ''theves'', ''thyeves'', ''thifes''), < AS. ''theóf'' (pl. ''theófas'') = OS. ''thiof'' = OFries. ''thiaf'', ''tief'' = D. ''dief'' = MLG. ''dēf'' = OHG. ''diob'', MHG. ''diep'', G. ''dieb'' = Icel. ''thiōfr'' = Sw. ''tjuf'' = Dan. ''tyv'' = Goth. ''thiufs'' (''thiub-''), thief: root unknown. Hence ''thieve'', ''theft''.] 1. A person who steals, or is guilty of larceny or robbery; one who takes the goods or property of another without the owner's knowledge or consent; especially, one who deprives another of property secretly or without open force, as opposed to a ''robber'', who openly uses violence.

{{smaller block|In the authorized version of the Bible, however, and in the older literature generally, ''thief'' is used where we now say ''robber''.}}

{{smaller block|The othre byeth the little ''thyeues'', thet steleth ine the house bread, wyn, an othre thinges.
{{right|''Ayenbite of Inwyt'' (E. E. T. S.), p. 38.}}}}

{{smaller block|A certain man went down from Jerusalem to Jericho, and fell among ''thieves'', which stripped him of his raiment.
{{right|Luke x. 30.}}}}

{{smaller block|Draw forth thy weapon, we are beset with ''thieves''.
{{right|''Shak.'', T. of the S., iii. 2. 238.}}}}

{{smaller block|The class that was called "travelling ''thieves'', " who, without being professional cracksmen, would creep into an unprotected house or rob a hen-roost.
{{right|''Nineteenth Century'', XXVI. 771.}}}}

2. A person guilty of cunning or deceitful acts; a lawless person; an evil-doer: used in reproach.

{{smaller block|Angelo is an adulterous ''thief''. {{right|''Shak.'', M. for M., v. 1. 40.}}}}

3. An imperfection in the wick of a candle, causing it to gutter. [Prov. Eng.]

{{smaller block|Where you see a ''thief'' in the candle, call presently for an extinguisher. {{right|''Bp. Hall'', Remains, p. 46. (''Latham.'')}}}}

{{smaller block|If there bee a ''theefe'' in the Candle (as wee used to say}}

readings.c2s3:
{{smaller block|commonly), there is a way to pull it out, and not to put out the Candle, by clapping an Extinguisher presently upon it. {{right|''Howell'', Forreine Travell, 1642 (ed. Arber), p. 77.}}}}

4. A tin can to which a small line or becket is attached, used as a drinking-cup by sailors. It is made heavier on one side, so that it will capsize when it is dropped in the water.—5. A thief-tube.—6. Same as ''hermit-crab.'' [Local, U. S.] {{smaller|—'''Bait-thief,''' a fish that takes the bait from a hook without getting caught. [Fishermen's slang.]—'''Thieves' Latin.''' See ''Latin.''—'''Thieves' vinegar,''' a kind of vinegar made by digesting rosemary-tops, sage-leaves, etc., in vinegar, formerly believed to be an antidote against the plague. It derived its name and popularity from a story that four thieves who plundered the dead during the plague ascribed their impunity to this infusion. It has been long disused as worthless.='''Syn.''' ''Pilferer, Pirate'' (see ''robber''), pickpocket, cutpurse. See ''pillage, n.''}}

'''thief'''<sup>2</sup> (thēf), ''n.'' [< ME. ''theve,'' < AS. ''thēfe,'' the bramble: see ''theve, theve-thorn.''] The bramble ''Rubus fruticosus.'' Compare ''theve-thorn. Britten and Holland.'' [Prov. Eng.]

'''thief-catcher''' (thēf′kach″ėr), ''n.'' One who catches thieves, or whose business is to detect thieves and bring them to justice.

{{smaller block|My evenings all I would with sharpers spend,<br>And make the ''thief-catcher'' my bosom friend.
{{right|''Bramston.''}}}}

'''thief-leader''' (thēf′lē″dėr), ''n.'' One who leads away or takes a thief. [Rare.]

{{smaller block|A wolf passed by as the ''thief-leaders'' were dragging a fox to execution. {{right|''Sir R. L'Estrange.''}}}}
